        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>What is tapas in the Ramayana?</summary><p class="faq-a">Tapas is intense, disciplined austerity practiced to gain spiritual power. In the Ramayana, Ravana performs severe tapas to please Brahma, who grants him a boon. Tellings differ in detail, so this article follows the popular retelling.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What can founders learn from Ravana's tapas?</summary><p class="faq-a">Two things. First, the discipline: long stretches of focused work before the market gives feedback, willingness to let go of ego, and building something hard to copy. Second, the warning: Ravana dismissed humans and monkeys as threats and was undone by exactly that blind spot.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is a startup moat?</summary><p class="faq-a">A moat is a durable advantage that makes it hard for competitors to copy or displace you, such as proprietary technology, high switching costs or network effects. A feature a funded copycat can rebuild quickly is not a moat.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Why do market leaders ignore small competitors?</summary><p class="faq-a">Leaders tend to measure themselves against peers they already know and dismiss newcomers that look small, unpolished or unprofitable. Blockbuster, Kodak and Nokia are often cited examples of incumbents that were slow to respond to different models.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How do founders avoid hubris?</summary><p class="faq-a">Build habits that bring outside signals in: regular customer conversations, a review of small and unusual competitors each quarter, advisors who disagree with you, and a rule that no segment is too small to study.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is this article religious advice?</summary><p class="faq-a">No. It uses a well-known story as a metaphor for business strategy and does not make religious claims. Versions of the Ramayana vary by region and tradition.</p></details>
        </div><p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-dim);line-height:1.7;margin-top:2rem;">This article uses a well-known story as a business metaphor. Ramayana tellings vary by region and tradition, and the company examples are simplified summaries of widely reported business histories.</p>
